svg.metadata:
sibling-of: works/everline-components-master.svg (2026-07-19), works/everline-components-batch2.svg (2026-07-20), works/everline-components-batch3.svg (2026-07-27) — independent batch, not a supersession of any of them;
scope: batch 4 (4 components) — Slider (issue #9), Accordion (issue #10), Popover (issue #11), Date / Time picker (issue #12). Created 2026-07-30 per batch4-plan.tmp.md's decision to split the remaining four batch-3 issues into their own sheet instead of appending them to works/everline-components-batch3.svg, which now holds only its own graduated components 1-4/8. NOTE: batch3.svg's file-level metadata and docs/design-system-v0.1-batch3-draft.md still say issues #9-#12 will be appended to batch 3 — that clarification is a stage-0 edit deliberately NOT made in this pass (this task was scoped to the SVG candidate stage only, and the working tree has unrelated uncommitted changes), so it remains an open inconsistency to fix before batch 4 proceeds past G1;
status: 4/4 graduated (2026-07-30). All four groups (c-slider, c-accordion, c-popover, c-date-time-picker) passed human visual review (gate G1) on 2026-07-30 and have since been reconciled against tokens and ported to HTML/CSS. This entire sheet is now a FROZEN historical snapshot and must not be updated further; superseded-by: works/html/batch4/index.html, which is the candidate spec authority for all four components. Two visual decisions were made by the human DURING that review and are reflected in this file: the Slider's focus indicator became a 4px solid ring with no gap (an earlier 2px stroke + 2px offset read as a detached hairline at 24px), and the date range's in-between days became individual tinted circles instead of one continuous band. Unlike batch 3, batch 4 was drawn as one sheet and reviewed in one pass, so there are no active un-drawn groups left here;
spec-reference: docs/design-system-v0.1-batch4-draft.md — records the spec, the token mapping, the deviations from this sheet, and the measurement results;
known-deviation: the HTML/CSS widened the time field from the 128px implied here to 160px. This sheet drew a static string (14:30), but a real input[type=time] renders in the user's locale (上午 09:00 under zh-TW) and needs 104px of intrinsic width. The token was corrected to 160px; this sheet was deliberately NOT redrawn, because a graduated sheet is a historical record of what was approved, not a live document;
token-status: this sheet uses NO component.slider.*, component.accordion.*, component.popover.* or component.date-time-picker.* tokens, because none exist yet. Every size and color drawn here is either (a) the resolved value of an existing token in tokens/everline-draft.tokens.json, or (b) a 校準候選 raw value called out in each group's 校準候選 caption. This follows the same token-before-use order batch 3 used: raw values with a calibration caption until human review passes, tokens formalized only afterward. No new color has been introduced — every fill and stroke below resolves to color.base.canvas #1A1A1A, gray-900 #333333, gray-800 #444444, gray-700 #4D4D4D, gray-600 #666666, off-white #F2F2F2, white #FFFFFF, blue #598AE8, or a documented opacity multiplier applied to one of them;
overlay-note: Popover's panel and the Date / Time picker's calendar panel both use color.semantic.background-overlay (gray-700 #4D4D4D), the shared floating-panel surface already used by Select, Split-button menu, Menu, Tooltip and Modal — as required by batch4-plan.tmp.md section 8.3. Consequence worth flagging at review: color.semantic.foreground-subdued resolves to the SAME gray-700, so subdued text is invisible on these panels. Muted text inside overlay panels therefore uses gray-600 #666666, which is the existing precedent set by component.menu.shortcut-foreground and component.menu.disabled-foreground, not a new value;
dependency-note: issue #12 (Date / Time picker) composes issue #11 (Popover). Per batch4-plan.tmp.md section 9.1, #12 may only proceed past the SVG stage after #11 has fully graduated; the two are drawn together here only so the composition can be reviewed as one visual decision, which does not shorten that dependency.
